WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.395
Lesson 0.1.

00:00:02.395 --> 00:00:05.362
Money, ledgers and why blockchains exist.

00:00:05.362 --> 00:00:07.893
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able

00:00:07.893 --> 00:00:10.785
to explain in plain words what a blockchain is,

00:00:10.785 --> 00:00:13.677
what a cryptocurrency is, and what "DeFi" means.

00:00:14.977 --> 00:00:16.267
Here's the plan.

00:00:16.267 --> 00:00:17.952
First, the key ideas.

00:00:17.952 --> 00:00:20.667
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:22.192
Then, your checklist.

00:00:22.192 --> 00:00:24.354
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:25.654 --> 00:00:28.469
First, money is a record of who owns what.

00:00:28.469 --> 00:00:30.224
Your bank balance is a line in

00:00:30.224 --> 00:00:32.844
your bank's private ledger (record book).

00:00:32.844 --> 00:00:34.999
You trust the bank to keep it correct.

00:00:34.999 --> 00:00:38.146
Next, A blockchain is a ledger that thousands of

00:00:38.146 --> 00:00:41.464
computers keep a copy of and agree on together.

00:00:41.464 --> 00:00:44.661
New entries are added in batches called blocks, each

00:00:44.661 --> 00:00:47.616
linked to the one before: a chain of blocks.

00:00:47.616 --> 00:00:50.116
Nobody can quietly change an old entry,

00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:53.166
because everyone else's copy would disagree.

00:00:53.166 --> 00:00:54.993
Then, A cryptocurrency is a

00:00:54.993 --> 00:00:57.251
unit recorded on a blockchain.

00:00:57.251 --> 00:01:00.341
Bitcoin (BTC) was the first (2009).

00:01:00.341 --> 00:01:02.635
Ether (ETH) is the currency of the

00:01:02.635 --> 00:01:05.951
Ethereum blockchain, which also runs programs.

00:01:05.951 --> 00:01:09.170
Also, A smart contract is a program that lives

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:12.801
on a blockchain and follows fixed rules, for example

00:01:12.801 --> 00:01:16.320
"whoever deposits X gets Y back plus interest".

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.845
On top of that, Stablecoins are tokens designed

00:01:20.845 --> 00:01:24.445
to stay worth $1 (e.g. USDC, USDT).

00:01:24.445 --> 00:01:26.147
You'll use them a lot, but

00:01:26.147 --> 00:01:27.850
they can fail (Lesson 1.5).

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:34.221
And DeFi (decentralised finance) is financial services (trading, lending,

00:01:34.221 --> 00:01:38.495
earning interest) run by smart contracts instead of banks.

00:01:38.495 --> 00:01:41.122
And finally, the trade-off: no bank in the

00:01:41.122 --> 00:01:43.993
middle means nobody can freeze your money, but

00:01:43.993 --> 00:01:46.615
also nobody can reverse your mistakes.

00:01:46.615 --> 00:01:48.810
That's why this program starts with safety.

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:52.625
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:52.625 --> 00:01:55.111
When Alice sends Bob 0.1 ETH,

00:01:55.111 --> 00:01:57.400
she doesn't send a file.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.094
She signs an instruction ("move 0.1

00:02:00.094 --> 00:02:02.713
ETH from my address to Bob's").

00:02:02.713 --> 00:02:04.781
The network checks she owns it, adds

00:02:04.781 --> 00:02:06.569
it to the next block, and every

00:02:06.569 --> 00:02:08.378
copy of the ledger updates.

00:02:08.378 --> 00:02:09.863
Bob now owns it.

00:02:09.863 --> 00:02:11.378
There is no "undo" button.

00:02:12.679 --> 00:02:14.139
Here's your checklist.

00:02:14.139 --> 00:02:15.674
Do it now, for real.

00:02:15.674 --> 00:02:18.023
First, I can explain a blockchain as

00:02:18.023 --> 00:02:20.989
"a shared ledger nobody can quietly edit".

00:02:20.989 --> 00:02:23.587
Next, I know the difference between

00:02:23.587 --> 00:02:25.764
BTC, ETH and a stablecoin.

00:02:25.764 --> 00:02:27.662
And finally, I understand that

00:02:27.662 --> 00:02:29.989
crypto transactions can't be reversed.

00:02:31.289 --> 00:02:32.499
Question 1.

00:02:32.499 --> 00:02:35.604
Why can't someone quietly change an old blockchain entry?

00:02:40.024 --> 00:02:43.100
The answer: Thousands of computers hold copies and

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:45.634
would reject a version that doesn't match.

00:02:46.934 --> 00:02:48.179
Question 2.

00:02:48.179 --> 00:02:50.194
What is a stablecoin designed to do?

00:02:54.614 --> 00:02:58.604
The answer: Stay worth about $1 (or another currency).

00:02:58.604 --> 00:02:59.739
It can still fail.

00:03:01.039 --> 00:03:02.309
Question 3.

00:03:02.309 --> 00:03:03.852
What does DeFi replace, and

00:03:03.852 --> 00:03:04.954
what do you give up?

00:03:09.374 --> 00:03:11.246
The answer: It replaces banks

00:03:11.246 --> 00:03:13.606
and brokers with smart contracts.

00:03:13.606 --> 00:03:15.269
You give up anyone who can

00:03:15.269 --> 00:03:17.671
reverse mistakes or help recover funds.

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:20.215
Let's recap.

00:03:20.215 --> 00:03:22.625
Money is a record of who owns what.

00:03:22.625 --> 00:03:25.893
A blockchain is a ledger that thousands of computers

00:03:25.893 --> 00:03:28.475
keep a copy of and agree on together.

00:03:28.475 --> 00:03:32.085
A cryptocurrency is a unit recorded on a blockchain.

00:03:32.085 --> 00:03:34.655
A smart contract is a program that lives

00:03:34.655 --> 00:03:37.162
on a blockchain and follows fixed rules.

00:03:38.463 --> 00:03:40.978
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:40.978 --> 00:03:43.251
Next up, Lesson 0.2: Opening

00:03:43.251 --> 00:03:45.838
and securing an exchange account.
